Subject: The layout export answers whether a function's apply is the builtin

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_apply_is_builtin(fn)` answers whether reading `fn.apply` would return the builtin
    `Function.prototype.apply`, without running JavaScript, allocating or taking a reference:
    the function's own properties first, then each prototype in turn, every object on the way
    must be a plain ordinary object, and the property must be a plain data property whose value
    is the builtin. It answers 0 for an own `apply` on the function, a replaced
    `Function.prototype.apply`, a getter, a Proxy, a bound function's exotic handler, a non-object
    value, and for the chain ending without `apply` (a depth bound of 16 is answered 0 too).

Why we need it
  A program that writes `f.apply(this, arguments)` pays the builtin's argument-list build for a
  call whose arguments are already a contiguous window. Bypassing that call is only sound while
  the `apply` the body would read is the builtin, and an own `apply` installed on one class's
  method, or a replaced `Function.prototype.apply`, must keep making its own call.
  `js_args_apply_is_builtin` tests a value the caller has already loaded, but a guard cannot load
  the property with `JS_GetProperty`: that can run a getter and can allocate, neither of which a
  guard may do.

Functions touched
  `qjs_aot_apply_is_builtin` (new, AOT builds only); `quickjs-aot-rt.h` declares it.

No (read-only; nothing changes behaviour).

--- a/quickjs-aot-rt.h
+++ b/quickjs-aot-rt.h
@@ -216,0 +217,6 @@
+/* Whether reading `fn.apply` would return the builtin `Function.prototype.apply`: the property
+   is found by walking the function's own properties and then its prototypes, every object on the
+   way must be a plain ordinary object, and the property must be a plain data property whose value
+   is the builtin. A getter is never run, nothing is allocated, and no reference is taken. A unit
+   that bypasses `f.apply(this, arguments)` asks this first and keeps the program's own call on 0. */
+QJS_AOT_STUB int qjs_aot_apply_is_builtin(JSValue fn);
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -64180,0 +64181,41 @@
+/* Whether reading `fn.apply` would hand back exactly the builtin `Function.prototype.apply`,
+   decided without running JavaScript, allocating or taking a reference. The property is looked
+   up the way an ordinary read would find it -- the function's own properties first, then each
+   prototype in turn -- and the walk stops with 0 as soon as an object on the way is not a plain
+   ordinary object, or holds `apply` as anything but a plain data property. A getter, a Proxy, a
+   bound function's exotic handler, an own `apply` and a replaced `Function.prototype.apply` all
+   answer 0, and the caller then makes the call the program wrote. */
+QJS_AOT_STUB int qjs_aot_apply_is_builtin(JSValue fn)
+{
+    JSObject *p;
+    JSProperty *pr;
+    JSShapeProperty *prs;
+    int depth;
+
+    if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(fn) != JS_TAG_OBJECT)
+        return 0;
+    p = JS_VALUE_GET_OBJ(fn);
+    for (depth = 0; depth < 16 && p != NULL; depth++) {
+        if (p->is_exotic)
+            return 0;
+        prs = find_own_property(&pr, p, JS_ATOM_apply);
+        if (prs != NULL) {
+            JSValue v;
+            JSObject *f;
+
+            if ((prs->flags & JS_PROP_TMASK) != JS_PROP_NORMAL)
+                return 0;
+            v = pr->u.value;
+            if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(v) != JS_TAG_OBJECT)
+                return 0;
+            f = JS_VALUE_GET_OBJ(v);
+            return f->class_id == JS_CLASS_C_FUNCTION &&
+                   f->u.cfunc.cproto == JS_CFUNC_generic_magic &&
+                   f->u.cfunc.c_function.generic_magic == js_function_apply &&
+                   f->u.cfunc.magic == 0;
+        }
+        p = p->shape->proto;
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
+
